import { telHref, usDigits } from "./phone"

/**
 * What a QR code holds, and how it is styled — MTC-FCS-020.
 * ============================================================================
 * Ported from the qrcode-king prototype, minus everything to do with tracking,
 * dashboards and campaigns. What is left is the part a contractor actually
 * uses: point it somewhere, make it look like theirs, download it.
 *
 * ── THE OPTIONS ARE BUILT HERE, NOT IN THE COMPONENT ───────────────────────
 * `qr-code-styling` only runs in a browser, so the component that drives it is
 * necessarily full of refs and effects. Keeping the *decisions* — what gets
 * encoded, which shapes are offered, what a preset means — out here means they
 * can be read and checked without a DOM.
 *
 * ── A QR CODE THAT DOES NOT OPEN IS WORSE THAN NO QR CODE ──────────────────
 * It is printed on a van. So `encodeValue` is deliberate about the things that
 * silently fail when scanned: a website with no scheme is treated as a
 * relative path by most scanners and opens nothing, a phone number needs the
 * `tel:` prefix, and an email needs `mailto:`. Those are added here rather
 * than left to whoever typed the field.
 */

